<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n27367" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Lȳdē</span></b> ,
		ēs, f., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Λύδη</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>the wife of the poet Antimachus of Claros, who
				attempted to console himself for her death by an elegiac poem
				which he named Lyde</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Clario Lyde dilecta poëtae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Ov.
					Tr. 1, 6, 1</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>The name of a female quack-doctor</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">turgida condīta pyxide Lyde,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Juv.
					2, 141</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
